            "blocks": [
              {
                "tag": "p",
                "content": [
                  "Inclusive design\nDefinition\n\nDesigning products that can be accessed by a diverse range of people\nwithout the need for any ",
                  {
                    "number": 31
                  },
                  ".\n\nNot the same as universal design: that is design for everyone, including\ncatering for people with ",
                  {
                    "number": 32
                  },
                  " problems\nExamples of inclusive design\n\n",
                  {
                    "number": 33
                  },
                  " which are adjustable, avoiding back or neck problems\n\n",
                  {
                    "number": 34
                  },
                  " in public toilets which are easier to use\nTo assist the elderly:\n\nDesigners avoid using ",
                  {
                    "number": 35
                  },
                  " in interfaces\n\nPeople can make commands using a mouse, keyboard or their ",
                  {
                    "number": 36
                  },
                  "\nImpact of non-inclusive designs\n\nAccess\no Loss of independence for disabled people.\n\nSafety\no Seatbelts are especially problematic for ",
                  {
                    "number": 37
                  },
                  " women.\no PPE jackets are often unsuitable because of the size of women’s\n",
                  {
                    "number": 38
                  },
                  ".\no PPE for female ",
                  {
                    "number": 39
                  },
                  " officers dealing with emergencies\nis the worst.\n\nComfort in the workplace\no The ",
                  {
